Absorbed source
---------------
The relay plan's derivation, checks, shell commands, host-state parsing,
comparison and reports are absorbed from SparkRing's SIRCL relay plan
installer and route module:

- sparkring_sircl/routes.py      -> fabric_tool/model.py
- sparkring_sircl/fabric/plan.py -> fabric_tool/plan.py
- sparkring_sircl/fabric/commands.py -> fabric_tool/commands.py
- sparkring_sircl/fabric/state.py    -> fabric_tool/state.py
- sparkring_sircl/fabric/diff.py     -> fabric_tool/diff.py
- sparkring_sircl/fabric/ops.py      -> fabric_tool/ops.py
- sparkring_sircl/fabric/report.py   -> fabric_tool/report.py
- sparkring_sircl/fabric/mesh_marker.c -> mesh_marker.c (unchanged)

as of SparkRing branch codex/shared-transport-core, commit a4de4dd5. The
tool's layout inputs follow SparkRing's runtime rendering of the same plan
(runtime/host/relays.py: a whole cycle is one whole-ring group with
same-function relay egress; a path is a group on a ring one Spark longer,
with sibling egress). The topology-file format is net-sircl's
(spark_transport/transport-core/src/topology.c), parsed to the same defaults.

Measured constraints the absorbed derivation encodes (SparkRing's
measurements on the cycle-of-eight cabling, 2026-10-11): a relay's hairpin
queue holds 512 KiB, the device maximum, and cannot pause its sender; relay
egress through the sibling Socket Direct function lowered burst loss to
0.06 % at 2 MiB; queue pairs use flow label 0; tags are EtherTypes chosen by
destination address because the sender NIC computes the ICRC after the
RDMA-TX rewrite.
